content.text:
No. Using GPT-6 isn't overfitting. Overfitting means the agent fits the **public data and sites**, and the model is a separate question.

- **The model is part of our submission, not something the private dataset changes.** The private run uses whatever model we pick, so if we submit with GPT-6, it runs on GPT-6 there too. Being tuned to the model we actually use is fine.
- **The checks we added because of GPT-6's mistakes are generic safeguards:** values must have been on a page, a topic only closes with a message the agent opened, no giving up in the form, the gap check. Any model can invent a value or give up, and these checks protect against that on any website.
- **The Qwen run showed one real coupling:** our design assumes GPT-6's speed. The 30-second step timeout and the solver's one long answer suit GPT-6. With a slower model, the same design ran out of time. That's a dependency on the model's speed, not on the public dataset. It's also why shrinking the calls matters: smaller calls make us less sensitive to model speed and to slower private sites.

**Where overfitting does exist is the dataset side:**
- fixed site roles (mail, ERP);
- the brief's topic wording;
- the five hydraulic attributes;
- timings tuned for 5 positions;
- the form's position-number format.

The generalization audit I just started covers exactly these. It imagines concrete private variants, traces them through the code and proposes generic fixes that keep the public 100. Both audits are running; I'll bring you one combined plan.

